  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper low currently rotating over WY will push out into South\nDakota and Nebraska tonight, weakening and moving into the \nUpper Midwest tomorrow. Some high clouds from this system for \nour area, but all models keep most of the precipitation to our \nsouth. At the surface, center of high pressure over Ontario \nwill move only very slightly east, allowing light east to \nsoutheast surface winds over our CWA. While can't completely \nrule out some fog formation tonight, it will be hampered by \nhigher clouds and a little bit of mixing. Right now best chances\nare up against the escarpment or Turtle mountains where the \nsoutheasterly winds will bring some upslope. The upper low pulls\noff to the east and leaves blocky ridging over the region \nthrough Tuesday. Upper flow starts to become more southwesterly \nWednesday, with one shortwave coming out into the Northern \nPlains late Wednesday and into Thursday. There is decent \nconsensus on southwesterly flow with various weak shortwaves \ncontinuing into next weekend. Can't really pin down \nprecipitation at this point, but temps look like they will be \nabove seasonal averages.\n\n...Frost chances for Monday night into Tuesday morning...\n\nWith the upper low moving east of our area by the first part of\nthe work week, clouds look to be decreasing for at least some\nperiod Monday night, and a bit drier air moving in from the\neast. Probability of temperatures below 36 degrees range from\nbelow 20 percent in most of the central and western CWA, but get\nup to around 40 to 50 percent along our far eastern border.\nAdvisory level temperatures dropping into the mid 30s are\npossible, although much will depend on exact cloud cover trends.\n\n...Next precipitation chances Wednesday night and Thursday...\n\nShortwave trough moving into the western Dakotas Wednesday will\nprogress quickly into the Great Lakes by Thursday. Instability\nis pretty weak at less than 500 J/kg, so severe is not expected\nalthough some thunderstorm activity is not completely out of the\nquestion. Main impacts look to be rain, with 40 to 60 percent\nprobabilities of over a quarter of an inch in portions of our\nwestern and southern counties.",
